{
    axi_sram:
    {
        Description: Verification plan for the AXI4 SRAM. Split across both block level and integration level tests.
        Node_Type: dut_name
        Block_level:
        {
            Description: Block-level verification of axi_sram. Tests both data and tag arrays.
            Node_Type: section_title
            Parameterization:
            {
                Node_Type: section_title
                interface_geometry:
                {
                    Reference: qohtih
                    Description: The SRAM block should have four ports: clock / reset / AXI4 request in / AXI4 response out
                    Node_Type: metric
                    Priority: 0
                    Comment: Not expressible as an assertion: the port list is fixed by the module declaration and any departure from it breaks every instantiation at compile time. Recorded as a review of the declaration instead
                    Items:
                    [
                        review:axi_sram.sv port list
                    ]
                }
                sram_geometry:
                {
                    Reference:
                        '''
                        jeluga
                        01skcc
                        u0s8nt
                        '''
                    Description: Confirm DUT parameterisation matches spec: AxiDataWidth = 64 (8-byte word), CapSize = 128-bit (16-byte capability), 1 CHERI tag bit per 128-bit aligned region,  SramMemSize = 128 KiB
                    Node_Type: metric
                    Priority: 0
                    Comment: SramMemSize 128 KiB, last word 0x1FFF8 and last cap slot 0x1FFF0 are derived from SRAMLength in top_pkg (0x2_0000) and 5scstu.
                    Items:
                    [
                        assert:axi_sram_assert.GeometryMatchesSpec_A
                        assert:axi_sram_assert.SramSizeMatchesSpec_A
                        assert:axi_sram.TagRamCoversDataRam_A
                        assert:axi_sram.SramSizeMatchesMap_A
                    ]
                }
            }
            Data_Path:
            {
                Description: Translation of AXI4 requests into the SRAM interface; data integrity
                Node_Type: section_title
                Clock_and_Reset:
                {
                    Description: Clock and reset input behaviour
                    Node_Type: section_title
                    rst_sanity:
                    {
                        Reference:
                            '''
                            947rwh
                            rrni5j
                            '''
                        Description: DUT should come out of reset cleanly and should accept the first transaction
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_rst_sanity
                        ]
                    }
                    rst_mid_burst:
                    {
                        Reference: rrni5j
                        Description: DUT is reset during an AXI burst
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Comment: Transaction should be dropped cleanly. The next transaction after an in-flight reset should still be accepted as normal. Would be good to clarification on the expected state of SRAM after a reset (i.e if a reset comes in during a 2-beat capability write)
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_rst_mid_burst
                        ]
                    }
                    init_value_undefined:
                    {
                        Reference: hqbiau
                        Description: Initial SRAM and tag contents are undefined at start-up; no read should be expected to return a known value before a write
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 2
                        Comment: The vseq reads an un-written location with no value expectation of its own, then checks the same location is deterministic after a write. The ref model returns 0 for un-written bytes, so the scoreboard does pin that first read to 0 - stricter than the requirement, but it matches the SRAM model's power-up contents
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_init_value_undefined
                        ]
                    }
                }
                Basic_access:
                {
                    Description: Single-beat aligned 64-bit read/write
                    Node_Type: section_title
                    write_read:
                    {
                        Reference:
                            '''
                            vknoin
                            pgo845
                            n5txiq
                            '''
                        Description: Single-beat 8-byte write followed by readback should return identical data
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_write_read
                        ]
                    }
                    address_boundaries:
                    {
                        Reference: u0s8nt
                        Description: Write/read first (0x0) and last valid word (0x1FFF8) and last capability slot (0x1FFF0)
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_address_boundaries
                        ]
                    }
                    data_all_bits:
                    {
                        Reference: vknoin
                        Description: Walk a 1 and a 0 through all 64 data bits to catch stuck-at faults
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_data_all_bits
                        ]
                    }
                    aligned_only:
                    {
                        Reference: lfcb7q
                        Description: Only aligned 64-bit accesses are exercised; misaligned data accesses are not expected/supported
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_aligned_only
                        ]
                    }
                }
                Bursts:
                {
                    Description: AXI4 burst handling
                    Node_Type: section_title
                    burst_last:
                    {
                        Reference:
                            '''
                            o02amt
                            mcykq8
                            '''
                        Description: Last beat of a burst should assert rlast and should expect wlast correctly
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_burst_last
                        ]
                    }
                    resp_id_match:
                    {
                        Reference: 4t4cew
                        Description: Response (B/R) AXI4 ID should equal the originating request ID
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Comment: Checked in the vseq, not the scoreboard: run_write and run_read compare BID and RID against the request ID when m_check_resp_id is set, which this testcase and multiple_outstanding_transactions turn on
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_resp_id_match
                        ]
                    }
                    atomic_excluded:
                    {
                        Reference: bsi4rc
                        Description: Atomic support is excluded, so an access carrying a non-zero awatop must be answered with a slave error rather than being serviced. Drive ATOP_ATOMICSTORE (awatop[5:4]=01), ATOP_ATOMICLOAD (10), ATOP_ATOMICSWAP (6'b110000) and ATOP_ATOMICCMP (6'b110001) at an in-range address and check that bresp is RESP_SLVERR (2'b10) with the request ID, that the atop[5] classes which also return read data report rresp RESP_SLVERR on every R beat with rlast on the final beat, that the addressed memory is left unchanged, and that the CHERI tag of the covering 128-bit slot is left unchanged because the rejected access must not write. A plain access to the same address afterwards must still succeed, so the error must not wedge the block
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Comment: Not testable with the current AXI agent: axi_if carries no AWATOP and axi_vip_if drives aw.atop as zero, so a non-zero ATOP cannot be driven at all. Needs AWATOP support in the agent first
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:TODO_atomic_excluded
                        ]
                    }
                    bounded_response:
                    {
                        Reference: 34ld5i
                        Description: Every request should receive a response within a bounded time proportional to burst length
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Comment: The bound is a fixed 32-cycle window rather than one scaled by burst length, and it is measured from the point the memory has everything it needs: BVALID after the last W beat is accepted, RVALID after the AR handshake. Only the first response beat is bounded; the later beats of a read burst are not
                        Items:
                        [
                            assert:axi_sram_assert.BValidBounded_A
                            assert:axi_sram_assert.RValidBounded_A
                        ]
                    }
                    backpressure:
                    {
                        Reference: NA
                        Description: Master side constrained random ready-throttling under load. the VIP holds r_ready/b_ready low with a random low duty cycle and injects valid-delays on the request channels (aw/w/ar), forcing the DUT's shallow internal FIFOs to fill and deassert its own aw_ready/w_ready/ar_ready. Data and CHERI tag must be intact once the master resumes.
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Comment: Response side only. The agent exposes READY timing on the B and R accepts, and the testcase draws both the duty cycle and the valid-to-ready delay randomly per transaction, but it exposes no delay on the manager's own AW/W/AR VALID, so the request channels cannot be throttled. That half needs request-channel pacing in the agent first
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_backpressure
                        ]
                    }
                }
                Concurrency:
                {
                    Description: Multiple AXI transactions in flight
                    Node_Type: section_title
                    concurrent_data_tag:
                    {
                        Reference: vknoin
                        Description: A plain-data write and a capability write issued concurrently should both complete and should read back correctly
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Comment: The two writes go out sequentially, not together: AXI4 forbids write-data interleaving, so two write sequences sharing the W channel would have to be coordinated to keep W in AW order and the agent does not do that. The reads are issued together on distinct IDs, though axi_to_detailed_mem accepts one read burst at a time, so they do not overlap on the bus either
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_concurrent_data_tag
                        ]
                    }
                    multiple_outstanding_transactions:
                    {
                        Reference: mcykq8
                        Description: multiple reads+writes to distinct addresses in flight should all complete, data+tag should be correct, and ids should match
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Comment: Nothing is ever outstanding together: axi_to_detailed_mem accepts one burst per direction and holds AxREADY low until it has been serviced, so the bridge is single-outstanding by construction. The test issues its eight reads together on distinct IDs, which is the most the DUT accepts, and the writes that set the data up go out sequentially; the overlap itself is unreachable
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_multiple_outstanding_transactions
                        ]
                    }
                    waw_same_address:
                    {
                        Reference: mcykq8
                        Description: back-to-back writes to the same address+tag should result in the last write data+tag being read back.
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_waw_same_address
                        ]
                    }
                    raw_war_same_region:
                    {
                        Reference: NA
                        Description: A read and a write to the same region both in flight concurrently. Exercises both RAW (the read may observe the region before or after the write lands) and WAR (the write lands while the read is still in flight).
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 2
                        Comment: Unreachable on this DUT: axi_to_detailed_mem arbitrates both directions onto one memory port and back-pressures AxREADY, so a read and a write to one region cannot be in flight together and no RAW/WAR hazard arises. The test issues them together and checks the settled result: the racing read returns either the pre-write or the post-write value whole, a subsequent ordered read returns the new data (RAW), and the final stored value equals the write data (WAR). The per-beat invariants a real hazard would need -- each beat independently a clean old or new value, with ruser matching the snapshot that beat saw -- are not checked, because the two beats of a capability read are separate memory accesses that a write may land between.
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_raw_war_same_region
                        ]
                    }
                }
            }
            CHERI_tags:
            {
                Description: CHERI tag bit storage and read-back behaviour (1 bit per 128-bit region; communicated via wuser/ruser)
                Node_Type: section_title
                Tag_write:
                {
                    Description: Conditions under which a tag is set to 1
                    Node_Type: section_title
                    tag_write:
                    {
                        Reference: 01skcc
                        Description: Each data field should have a corresponding tag field which should be accessible in the same transaction
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_tag_write
                        ]
                    }
                    no_tag_single_beat:
                    {
                        Reference:
                            '''
                            8rlwol
                            35vdeg
                            '''
                        Description: wuser=1 on a single-beat (awlen=0) write must NOT set the tag (not a full region in a single burst)
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Comment: Corner: tag only on full capability burst
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_no_tag_single_beat
                        ]
                    }
                    no_tag_misaligned:
                    {
                        Reference: 8rlwol
                        Description: 2-beat wuser=1 burst starting at a non-16-byte-aligned address must NOT set the tag (is_w_cap_aligned requires addr[3:0]==0)
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Comment: Corner: alignment gating
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_no_tag_misaligned
                        ]
                    }
                    no_tag_two_bursts:
                    {
                        Reference:
                            '''
                            35vdeg
                            8rlwol
                            '''
                        Description: Writing both halves of a 128-bit region as two separate single-beat bursts must NOT set the tag; the tag requires a single 2-beat capability burst
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Comment: Corner: full region covered across two separate bursts still leaves the tag 0
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_no_tag_two_bursts
                        ]
                    }
                    assert_wuser_not_full_cap:
                    {
                        Reference: bj8we7
                        Description: Assertion should fire when wuser=1 on a write that is not part of a full capability write
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Comment: The assertion covers the strobe dimension only. It fires on the tag write itself (cheri_w_tag implies every byte strobed), and for a single-beat or a misaligned write the RTL has already cleared cheri_w_tag, so those shapes cannot reach it; they are covered by no_tag_single_beat, no_tag_misaligned and no_tag_two_bursts instead
                        Items:
                        [
                            assert:axi_sram_assert.TagSetNeedsFullStrobe_A
                            testcase:axi_sram_wuser_partial_strobe
                        ]
                    }
                    assert_wuser_mismatch:
                    {
                        Reference:
                            '''
                            9a3xf6
                            8rlwol
                            '''
                        Description: A mismatched wuser pair is not a full capability write, so it must never leave a set tag. Drive both beat orders of an otherwise well-formed 2-beat aligned capability write explicitly - wuser = (1, 0) and wuser = (0, 1) - and for each check that the assertion fires and, separately, that a readback of the covering 128-bit slot returns a cleared tag. The readback is the part that still holds when assertions are disabled around deliberate error injection
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Issue: https://github.com/lowRISC/mocha/issues/717
                        Comment: The (0, 1) order stores a set tag today. The tag RAM is written on every beat and both beats of a 2-beat capability write, so the stored value is the last beat's cheri_w_tag, and reduces to the last beat's wuser for an aligned, correctly sized burst. This results in a forged capability tag on a write that was never a full capability write. Stated against the architectural requirement, so the testpoint stays valid whatever the RTL currently does. There is no assertion for the mismatch: nothing in the SVA compares the two beats' wuser, so the readback in the testcase is the whole check
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_wuser_mismatch
                        ]
                    }
                    cg_tag_write:
                    {
                        Reference:
                            '''
                            8rlwol
                            35vdeg
                            893tz4
                            '''
                        Description: Coverage of the tag-write gating conditions (burst length/size, alignment, strobe, wuser), each crossed with the tag bit.
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Items:
                        [
                            functional:axi_sram_cov::cg_tag_write
                        ]
                    }
                }
                Tag_clear:
                {
                    Description: Conditions under which a previously-set tag is cleared
                    Node_Type: section_title
                    tag_cleared_by_write:
                    {
                        Reference: 893tz4
                        Description: A plain data write to either word of a tagged capability slot should clear the tag for the whole region
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Comment: Corner: any non-cap write zeroes the tag bit
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_tag_cleared_by_write
                        ]
                    }
                    partial_strobe_clears_tag:
                    {
                        Reference: 893tz4
                        Description: A sub-64-bit (partial write-strobe) write to a tagged slot should clear the tag
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Comment: Corner: partial strobe clears tag
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_partial_strobe_clears_tag
                        ]
                    }
                    subword_read_clears_tag:
                    {
                        Reference:
                            '''
                            raa5pw
                            832lpx
                            '''
                        Description: Reads of only part of a 64-bit value from a valid cap region should return data but with the tag cleared
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Comment: Tag is returned as clear in the response, but not modified in memory.
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_subword_read_clears_tag
                        ]
                    }
                }
                Tag_read:
                {
                    Description: Per-beat ruser (CHERI tag) generation on reads
                    Node_Type: section_title
                    cap_both_ruser_set:
                    {
                        Reference:
                            '''
                            kn6exz
                            af8sx6
                            u95b14
                            '''
                        Description: Both ruser flits of a valid capability read should be 1
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Comment: the SRAM invalidate a cap by clearing one or both `ruser` bits, so the two flits are not guaranteed equal by spec. Rather than treat the asymmetric (1,0) case as not-applicable, bind an SVA that on every 2-beat capability read both flits are equal and match the single stored tag bit (ruser_flit0 == ruser_flit1). This proves the one-bit-per-region design never emits a false-valid asymmetric result.
                        Items:
                        [
                            assert:axi_sram_assert.CapReadRuserBeatsAgree_A
                            testcase:axi_sram_cap_ruser
                        ]
                    }
                    burst_read_mixed_tags:
                    {
                        Reference: kn6exz
                        Description: A read spanning a tagged and an untagged capability slot should return the correct per-beat ruser for each
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 2
                        Comment: The spanning read is logged, not checked. axi_sram gates ruser on arlen==1, so every beat of a longer burst returns 0 and per-beat tags across such a burst are the tag controller's job, not this block's. What the testcase checks is the two slots read as their own 2-beat capability reads, each returning its own tag
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_burst_read_mixed_tags
                        ]
                    }
                    misaligned_read_clears_tag:
                    {
                        Reference: NA
                        Description: A 2-beat 8-byte read that starts half way into a capability slot is capability-shaped but not capability-aligned, so it must return a cleared tag on both beats and must leave the stored tag alone
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Comment: The read-side counterpart of no_tag_misaligned. is_r_cap_aligned wants araddr[3:0]==0 on the first beat and ==8 on the last, so a burst that starts mid-slot reports no tag. The reference model carries the same rule, so the scoreboard checks it on every read of this shape, and the assertion holds it over all traffic rather than the shapes a test happens to issue
                        Items:
                        [
                            assert:axi_sram_assert.NonCapReadHasNoTag_A
                            testcase:axi_sram_misaligned_read_clears_tag
                        ]
                    }
                    cg_tag_read:
                    {
                        Reference:
                            '''
                            kn6exz
                            raa5pw
                            '''
                        Description: Coverage of the ruser tag returned on reads: the per-flit tag pair on capability reads, and cleared tags on sub-word reads
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 1
                        Items:
                        [
                            functional:axi_sram_cov::cg_tag_read
                        ]
                    }
                }
                Tag_isolation:
                {
                    Description: Tags are stored independently and in separate memory from data
                    Node_Type: section_title
                    tag_isolation:
                    {
                        Reference: 01skcc
                        Description: Writing/clearing the tag of one 128-bit slot must not affect an adjacent slot's tag
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_tag_isolation
                        ]
                    }
                    tag_aliasing:
                    {
                        Reference: 01skcc
                        Description: Every 128-bit slot across the whole 128 KiB must own a distinct tag bit, so slots that are far apart must not share tag storage. Write a valid capability at slot i, then a plain-data write at slot i+1024 (16 KiB higher), for several i spread over the address space, and check each slot's tag is unaffected by the other. Repeat with the order reversed
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 0
                        Comment: The 16 KiB stride is the regression check for the tag address width fix in #716
                        Items:
                        [
                            testcase:axi_sram_tag_aliasing
                        ]
                    }
                    tag_separate_memory:
                    {
                        Reference: lzoy40
                        Description: Tags should be stored in a separate memory block from data
                        Node_Type: metric
                        Priority: 2
                        Comment: Not expressible as an assertion: the separation is structural, two prim_ram_1p instances with no shared storage, so there is no run-time condition to check. Recorded as a review of the instantiations instead
                        Items:
                        [
                            review:axi_sram.sv u_ram / u_tag_ram
                        ]
                    }
                }
            }
            Stress_random:
            {
                Description: Randomised back-to-back access sweeps
                Node_Type: section_title
                Comment: randomization should be seeded for reproducibility
                random_data:
                {
                    Reference: NA
                    Description: N random (aligned addr, data) write/read pairs across the address space
                    Node_Type: metric
                    Priority: 1
                    Items:
                    [
                        testcase:axi_sram_random_data
                    ]
                }
                random_capabilities:
                {
                    Reference:
                        '''
                        8rlwol
                        kn6exz
                        '''
                    Description: N random 16-byte-aligned capability write/read pairs verifying data and tag
                    Node_Type: metric
                    Priority: 1
                    Items:
                    [
                        testcase:axi_sram_random_capabilities
                    ]
                }
            }
        }
        Integration:
        {
            Description: Verification of axi_sram integrated behind the real Mocha interconnect.
            Node_Type: section_title
            Address_map:
            {
                Reference: lhfz6r
                Description: Address decode through the real mocha AXI crossbar. Only the SRAM aperture (SRAMBase=0x10000000, 128 KiB) returns OKAY; every other address returns DECERR (the xbar has no default master port; non-SRAM device ports carry axi_err_slv). The scoreboard is decode-aware: in-aperture it checks data/tag, out-of-aperture it expects DECERR and flags any OKAY as a decode bug.
                Node_Type: section_title
                sram_reachable:
                {
                    Description: Accesses inside the SRAM range should reach axi_sram and should round-trip correctly through the interconnect
                    Node_Type: metric
                    Priority: 0
                    Comment: Covered by the first two round trips in sram_boundary, which write and read the first and last SRAM words through the crossbar. Every other integration testcase also round-trips SRAMBase as its control before checking a DECERR
                    Items:
                    [
                        testcase:axi_sram_sram_boundary
                    ]
                }
                out_of_range_error:
                {
                    Reference: u0s8nt
                    Description: Accesses outside every mapped SRAM region should return DECERR
                    Node_Type: metric
                    Priority: 1
                    Comment: Fully-out-of-range accesses result in an error; the partial-out-of-range part is enforced by proxy of AXI4 not allowing bursts to span a 4KB boundary
                    Items:
                    [
                        testcase:axi_sram_out_of_range_error
                        testcase:axi_sram_wrong_region_decerr
                    ]
                }
                burst_wrap:
                {
                    Reference: u0s8nt
                    Description: No burst on the SRAM port crosses a 4 KB boundary, so no burst starting in SRAM can have later beats leave the mapped range
                    Node_Type: metric
                    Priority: 2
                    Comment: axi_sram does not range-check (mem_err_i=0; it masks the address and would silently wrap), so range enforcement rests on master compliance. The SRAM region is 4 KB aligned at both ends, so the boundary assertion covers every burst that could leave it
                    Items:
                    [
                        assert:axi_sram_assert.AwNo4kbCrossing_A
                        assert:axi_sram_assert.ArNo4kbCrossing_A
                        testcase:axi_sram_burst_wrap
                    ]
                }
                sram_boundary:
                {
                    Reference: lhfz6r
                    Description: Address range edges: the first and last SRAM words should round-trip OK; the word just below SRAMBase and the word just past the top are unmapped and should return DECERR
                    Node_Type: metric
                    Priority: 1
                    Items:
                    [
                        testcase:axi_sram_sram_boundary
                    ]
                }
                execute_from_sram:
                {
                    Reference: lhjkel
                    Description: Instruction-flavoured reads (arprot[2]=1) should return stored words identically to data reads
                    Node_Type: metric
                    Priority: 2
                    Comment: only the arprot[2]=instruction dimension is SRAM-specific
                    Items:
                    [
                        testcase:axi_sram_execute_from_sram
                    ]
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
